  "informal": {
    "text": "Every magma law using at most four binary operations is semantically equivalent to an entry of the project law list.",
    "scope": "Coverage of the law enumeration, not a second independent implication-graph result; shares the certification family and split with ENDTOEND.",
    "source_file": "paper/intro.tex",
    "source_sha256": "897ba3fa9160ff5391fc852ad3655268c8c23977126ffdb9c2c02e8e0bd3a710",
    "source_url": "https://github.com/teorth/equational_theories/blob/8b87204d5b201e1cf6c69b8dcabb0558109bee00/paper/intro.tex",
    "source_locators": [
      {
        "line": 13,
        "text": "In the ETP, a unique number was assigned to each equational law, via a numbering system that we describe in \\Cref{numbering-app}.  For instance, the \\emph{commutative law} $\\x \\op \\y \\formaleq \\y \\op \\x$ is assigned to the equation number $\\Eq{43}$, while the \\emph{associative law} $\\x \\op (\\y \\op \\z) \\formaleq (\\x \\op \\y) \\op \\z$ is assigned to the equation $\\Eq{4512}$.  A list of all equations referred to by number in this paper is also provided in \\Cref{numbering-app}."
      },
      {
        "line": 37,
        "text": "As noted in \\Cref{numbering-app}, there are $\\num{4694}$ equational laws of order at most $4$. The primary mathematical goal of the ETP was to completely determine the \\emph{implication graph} for these laws, in which there is a directed edge from $\\E$ to ${\\E'}$ precisely when ${\\E} \\models {\\E'}$. As the project progressed, an additional goal was added to determine the slightly larger \\emph{finite implication graph}, in which there is a directed edge from $\\E$ to ${\\E'}$ precisely when ${\\E} \\modelsfin {\\E'}$."
      }
    ],
    "author_written_mathematical_source": true
  },
